
# ── Journey 1: Social Foundations ──────────────────────────────────────────
levels.append({'id':1,'journey_id':1,'journey_title':'Social Foundations','title':'Introducing Yourself',
'scenario':'You arrive at a professional networking event at a downtown conference centre. The room hums with conversation and the clink of glasses. Across the room a well-dressed stranger near the refreshments table catches your eye and offers a small smile. This is your moment to walk over, introduce yourself, and leave a memorable first impression.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You have just made eye contact with the stranger and they smiled at you. What is the best first move?',
'Walk over confidently, return the smile, and extend your hand for a handshake.',
['Stay where you are and wait for them to approach you first.','Look away quickly so you do not seem too eager.','Check your phone briefly so you appear relaxed before approaching.'],
'Taking the initiative signals confidence and openness. A warm smile and extended hand communicates that you are approachable and genuinely interested in connecting.',
'Waiting passively can cost you the opportunity. Looking away signals disinterest. Reaching for your phone right before approaching undercuts the sincerity of the moment.'),
mcq(2,'You have walked over and they have shaken your hand warmly. What do you say as your opening line?',
'Hi, I am Alex. Great event so far, isn\'t it? What brings you here today?',
['Hi, I am Alex, senior product manager with twelve years in B2B SaaS — let me tell you about my work.','Hi — do you know many people here? I barely know anyone.','So, what company are you with?'],
'Sharing your name, making a light observation about the shared environment, and asking an open question creates a natural opening that invites the other person to engage without pressure.',
'Leading with a full resume makes the exchange feel transactional. Opening with vulnerability about knowing no one creates awkwardness. Jumping straight to what company feels interrogative rather than conversational.'),
mcq(3,'They introduce themselves as Jamie and mention they work in marketing for a tech startup. How do you keep the conversation flowing?',
'That sounds exciting — what kind of products does your startup focus on?',
['Oh nice. Anyway, let me tell you about what I do — it is pretty interesting.','Marketing must be really tough right now with all the AI disruption, right?','I do not know much about marketing, so I might not be the best conversation partner for you.'],
'Asking a specific follow-up question about their work shows genuine curiosity and keeps the spotlight on them, which people find naturally engaging.',
'Pivoting to yourself immediately shuts down their story. Framing their field negatively can feel deflating. Dismissing your ability to connect pre-emptively closes the conversation before it starts.'),
mcq(4,'Jamie shares enthusiastically about their AI product. After a great few minutes the keynote is about to begin. How do you wrap up?',
'It has been fantastic talking with you, Jamie. I\'d love to stay in touch — shall we swap contact details before we head in?',
['Okay, I need to find my seat. See you around.','We should grab lunch tomorrow — are you free at noon?','Let me take a photo of your name tag so I remember you.'],
'Expressing genuine appreciation and suggesting an exchange of contact details closes warmly while preserving the relationship for follow-up.',
'An abrupt goodbye wastes the goodwill you built. Proposing lunch tomorrow can feel too forward too soon. Photographing a name tag is unusual and may feel invasive.'),
mcq(5,'Jamie hands you their business card and says they\'d love to keep in touch. What is the ideal way to receive and respond?',
'Accept the card with both hands, glance at it briefly, say "Thank you, Jamie — I\'ll reach out this week," and offer your own card in return.',
['Pocket it without looking at it and give a quick thanks.','Say "I\'ll just find you on LinkedIn" and hand the card back.','Read every detail on the card aloud while Jamie stands there waiting.'],
'Accepting the card respectfully, using their name, and committing to a concrete follow-up shows professionalism. Offering your own card completes the exchange gracefully.',
'Pocketing the card without acknowledgment feels dismissive. Rejecting the physical card in favour of LinkedIn feels impersonal. Reading it aloud in full creates a long, uncomfortable pause.')],
'response':resp('You navigated the full arc: approached confidently, introduced yourself warmly, asked genuine questions, and closed with a plan to reconnect. Now type or say aloud the exact closing line you would use as you turn to find your seat, leaving Jamie with a strong final impression.',
'Excellent! A warm, memorable closing line seals the positive impression you built and sets the stage for a meaningful follow-up. You are mastering the art of first introductions!')})

levels.append({'id':2,'journey_id':1,'journey_title':'Social Foundations','title':'Starting a Conversation',
'scenario':'You have joined a local book club for the first time. Most attendees seem to know each other well and the room is full of laughter and inside references you do not yet share. You spot someone sitting alone near the window quietly flipping through the selected book. They look just as new as you feel — this seems like the right moment to break the ice.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You see someone sitting alone at the book club. What is the best way to initiate contact?',
'Approach with a friendly smile and say, "Hi — mind if I join you? I\'m still finding my footing here."',
['Sit down next to them without saying a word and start reading your own copy.','Wave from across the room and wait to see if they come over.','Ask the host to formally introduce the two of you.'],
'A warm, low-key opener that acknowledges your shared newness immediately creates common ground and lowers the social barrier for both of you.',
'Sitting silently creates awkwardness. Waving from a distance puts the social burden on them. Relying on the host to introduce you is overly formal and may not happen at all.'),
mcq(2,'They smile and say they are new here too. How do you build on this shared experience?',
'Oh great, solidarity! Have you managed to read much of the book yet? I found the first chapter really thought-provoking.',
['Oh, I\'ve actually been twice before — so not totally new.','Yeah, being new is rough. I hope the group is welcoming.','I read the whole thing in one sitting — it was impossible to put down.'],
'Linking immediately to the shared topic of the book gives the conversation a natural anchor and invites them to share their perspective without pressure.',
'Correcting their assumption undercuts the moment of connection. Dwelling on the anxiety of being new keeps the conversation in a negative frame. Emphasising how much you\'ve read can unintentionally make them feel behind.'),
mcq(3,'They admit they only read half the book. How do you respond without making them feel embarrassed?',
'Honestly, life got in the way for me too — what did you think of what you did read?',
['You really should finish it before attending — it is a bit unfair to the group.','Don\'t worry, I\'ll fill you in on what happens at the end.','Oh wow, I feel bad now — I read it twice.'],
'Normalising the partial read removes shame and smoothly redirects to their opinion, keeping the dialogue open and equal.',
'Suggesting they were wrong to attend unfinished is judgmental. Volunteering a plot summary robs them of the reading experience. Mentioning you read it twice makes them feel worse by comparison.'),
mcq(4,'You have been chatting comfortably when the host calls everyone to gather. How do you handle this transition?',
'Let\'s pick this up after the discussion — I\'d love to hear more of your thoughts.',
['Continue talking quietly through the opening remarks so you do not lose momentum.','Stand up abruptly and join the group without saying anything.','Loudly tell the host that you and your new friend are in the middle of something.'],
'Signalling that you want to continue later is a natural, courteous pause that preserves the connection without disrupting the group.',
'Talking through the host\'s remarks is disrespectful to everyone else. Leaving without a word feels abrupt. Making a scene draws unwanted attention.'),
mcq(5,'After the discussion you reconnect and they say they really enjoyed talking with you. How do you close and leave the door open?',
'Same here! Would you be up for grabbing coffee before next month\'s meeting so we can chat more about the book?',
['Yes, me too! Well, bye then.','Great — I\'ll add you to the group chat I\'m already in with everyone else here.','We should definitely hang out — give me all your contact details.'],
'Suggesting a specific, low-pressure follow-up tied to a shared interest moves the connection forward naturally and shows you mean it.',
'A flat goodbye after they expressed warmth is a missed opportunity. Adding them to an existing group chat assumes too much. Asking for all your contact details feels overly eager.')],
'response':resp('You found a fellow newcomer, built genuine rapport over a shared experience, and closed with a plan to meet again. Now type or say aloud what you would say as you both put on your coats to leave — a warm, natural final line that cements the new friendship.',
'Well done! You turned a potentially awkward situation into the start of a real connection. Starting conversations with empathy and shared context is a skill that will serve you everywhere.')})

levels.append({'id':3,'journey_id':1,'journey_title':'Social Foundations','title':'Making Small Talk',
'scenario':'You are waiting for a delayed flight in a busy airport lounge. The person seated next to you closes their laptop with a sigh, glances at the departures board, and mutters something about the delay. You sense an opening for a light, friendly conversation to make the wait more pleasant for both of you.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'The person next to you sighs about the delay. What is the ideal opening for small talk?',
'Right? I was hoping to make my connection — where are you headed?',
['This airline is honestly the worst. I\'ve had nothing but problems with them.','I actually don\'t mind delays — gives me extra reading time.','Delays happen. Not much we can do about it.'],
'Validating their frustration briefly and immediately asking a light question about their destination opens a natural two-way exchange.',
'Launching into a rant sets a negative tone. Countering their frustration with positivity can feel dismissive. A fatalistic non-response gives them nothing to work with.'),
mcq(2,'They say they are flying to Barcelona for a friend\'s wedding. How do you keep the small talk going warmly?',
'Oh how lovely! Is it a big wedding? I\'ve always wanted to visit Barcelona.',
['Nice. I\'ve been to Barcelona — it\'s overrated honestly.','Weddings abroad are so expensive these days, aren\'t they?','That\'s nice. I\'m going to a business conference myself.'],
'Expressing genuine delight and following up with an easy question about the wedding invites them to share more about an exciting personal event.',
'Immediately deflating their excitement with a negative take is discouraging. Focusing on costs shifts to a negative frame. Redirecting to your own trip signals low interest in theirs.'),
mcq(3,'They light up talking about the wedding and ask if you like to travel. How do you respond in a way that keeps the conversation balanced?',
'I love it, though I don\'t get to do it as much as I\'d like. Any trips that have really stayed with you?',
['Yes, I\'ve been to 47 countries — want to hear about all of them?','Not really, travel makes me anxious.','Travel is great but it\'s terrible for the environment, so I try to limit it.'],
'Sharing briefly about yourself and returning the question keeps the exchange reciprocal and shows curiosity about their experiences rather than dominating.',
'Listing all your countries monopolises the conversation. A flat negative closes it down. Introducing a guilt-inducing topic like carbon footprint derails the light, pleasant tone.'),
mcq(4,'They share a wonderful story about a road trip through New Zealand. The gate agent announces boarding in ten minutes. How do you transition?',
'That sounds absolutely magical — New Zealand is on my list for sure. Sounds like we\'re nearly boarding; it was lovely chatting with you.',
['Oh, we\'re boarding soon? Sorry, I need to get my things ready — been nice.','Okay, just quickly — let me also tell you about my best trip before we board.','I should warn you, boarding is always chaotic on this airline.'],
'Reflecting their story positively and acknowledging the approaching end naturally closes the conversation on a high note.',
'A rushed dismissal undercuts the warmth of the exchange. Squeezing in your own story when time is short feels self-centred. Ending with a warning about the airline re-introduces the frustration you both moved past.'),
mcq(5,'As you both stand to join the boarding queue they say the wait was so much better for the chat. How do you respond?',
'Completely agree — enjoy every moment of the wedding! Safe travels.',
['Ha, no problem. I talk to strangers all the time.','It was nothing really — I was just killing time.','Yes, well — hopefully the flight makes up for the delay!'],
'Affirming the positive exchange and sending them off with a warm, specific wish for the wedding makes the goodbye genuinely memorable.',
'Mentioning you talk to strangers all the time makes the moment feel less special. Downplaying the interaction devalues the connection. Closing on the delay brings back the frustration unnecessarily.')],
'response':resp('You turned a frustrating airport delay into a warm, memorable conversation — staying positive, curious, and balanced throughout. Now type or say aloud one final thing you would call out as you both head down the jetbridge in different directions.',
'Wonderful! Great small talk leaves people feeling seen and uplifted. You kept the tone light, stayed curious, and closed with genuine warmth — the hallmarks of a natural conversationalist.')})

levels.append({'id':4,'journey_id':1,'journey_title':'Social Foundations','title':'Active Listening Basics',
'scenario':'Your friend calls you unexpectedly one evening. They had a rough day at work — their big project was criticised in a team meeting — and they clearly need to vent. You sense they are not looking for advice right now, just someone to truly hear them. Your goal is to be that listener.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'Your friend starts describing what happened at the meeting. What is the first thing you should do?',
'Put down whatever you are doing, give them your full attention, and say, "That sounds really frustrating — tell me what happened."',
['Listen while scrolling your phone, chipping in with "uh-huh" occasionally.','Immediately say, "I\'m sure it wasn\'t that bad — projects always get criticised."','Ask, "Have you spoken to your manager about it?" before they have finished explaining.'],
'Full physical and mental attention, combined with an invitation to share more, signals that you take their experience seriously and are truly present with them.',
'Half-listening while scrolling is detectable and feels dismissive. Minimising their experience before understanding it invalidates their feelings. Jumping to solutions before they finish cuts off their chance to be heard.'),
mcq(2,'They describe the criticism in detail and pause. How do you show that you have truly understood what they said?',
'So if I\'m hearing you right, the team felt the approach was off — but you had put a lot of thought into it and that feedback felt really unfair. Is that right?',
['Yeah, people at work can be so cruel. I had something similar happen to me last year.','I get it. So what are you going to do about it?','Well, maybe there was some truth to the criticism and it\'s worth considering?'],
'Paraphrasing and checking your understanding shows you were genuinely listening and gives them the chance to feel fully understood or to clarify.',
'Pivoting to your own story shifts the focus off them. Jumping to action mode ignores the emotional layer. Suggesting merit in the criticism, however gently, can feel invalidating before they feel heard.'),
mcq(3,'They confirm you understood and say they feel like they put in all this effort and nobody noticed. How do you respond empathetically?',
'That is genuinely hard — putting your best into something and feeling like it went unseen. That would frustrate anyone.',
['Welcome to the real world — that\'s just how corporate environments are.','Maybe you need to promote your work more visibly so people notice.','I\'ve felt that way too — let me tell you about a time that happened to me.'],
'Naming and normalising their emotion without judgment or advice validates their experience and helps them feel less alone in it.',
'Dismissing with "that\'s just how it is" feels cold. Offering unsolicited advice on self-promotion skips the emotional step they need. Switching to your own story redirects focus away from them.'),
mcq(4,'They seem a little lighter after venting and ask what you think they should do. How do you respond now that they have invited advice?',
'I think you have options — do you want me to brainstorm with you, or would it help more to talk through how you\'re feeling about it first?',
['Honestly, I would just redo the whole project from scratch.','You should send a strongly worded email to your manager tonight.','I think you should just let it go — it\'s not worth the energy.'],
'Asking whether they want to problem-solve or continue processing keeps them in control of what kind of support they need, which is what great listeners do.',
'Jumping to a specific bold action plan may not match what they need. Suggesting an emotionally charged email risks making things worse. Telling them to let it go dismisses the genuine hurt they expressed.'),
mcq(5,'They say they just needed to say it all out loud and they feel better. Thanks for listening. How do you close the conversation?',
'Of course — that\'s what I\'m here for. You worked hard on that project and that matters, regardless of today\'s feedback. You\'ve got this.',
['No problem — honestly it wasn\'t a big deal to listen.','Good. So what are you making for dinner tonight?','Glad you feel better. You really should have called earlier though.'],
'Affirming both your availability and their effort sends them off with genuine encouragement, closing on a note of warmth and confidence.',
'Minimising the act of listening devalues the support you gave. Pivoting abruptly to something trivial breaks the emotional close. Adding a mild criticism at the end undermines the supportive tone entirely.')],
'response':resp('You stayed fully present, paraphrased accurately, validated your friend\'s emotions, and only offered advice when invited. Now type or say aloud a final encouraging message you would send them as a text after hanging up the phone.',
'Beautiful listening! You gave your friend exactly what they needed — a space to feel truly heard. Active listening is one of the most powerful gifts you can offer anyone.')})

levels.append({'id':5,'journey_id':1,'journey_title':'Social Foundations','title':'Remembering Names',
'scenario':'You are at a company mixer where you are introduced to five new colleagues in rapid succession. Within the hour your manager asks you to include one of them — Priya who leads analytics — in an impromptu project discussion. You realise with a sinking feeling that you have already forgotten which person was Priya.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You have forgotten which person is Priya. What is the best way to handle this without embarrassing yourself or her?',
'Quietly ask a trusted colleague nearby if they could point out Priya from analytics for you.',
['Walk up to the group and loudly say "Wait, which one of you is Priya again?"','Avoid the situation entirely and tell your manager you couldn\'t find her.','Guess based on who looks like they might work in analytics and hope for the best.'],
'Discreetly asking a colleague spares everyone potential awkwardness and resolves the situation efficiently and professionally.',
'Asking publicly can embarrass Priya and reflect poorly on you. Avoiding the task lets your manager down. Guessing risks approaching the wrong person and creating a worse situation.'),
mcq(2,'You have located Priya and are walking toward her. She is mid-conversation with someone else. How do you approach?',
'Wait nearby, make brief eye contact with her, and smile patiently until there is a natural pause in her conversation.',
['Tap her on the shoulder and interrupt immediately.','Stand right next to her and start speaking before the other conversation ends.','Send her a message on the company chat app instead of approaching in person.'],
'Waiting for a natural break and signalling your presence with eye contact and a smile is respectful of both her time and the ongoing conversation.',
'Interrupting mid-conversation is rude. Starting to speak before the other exchange ends is equally disruptive. Resorting to messaging when you are standing nearby is unnecessary and impersonal.'),
mcq(3,'She turns to you and smiles. How do you greet her in a way that reinforces her name in your memory?',
'Hi Priya, I\'m [Name] — I think we were briefly introduced earlier. I\'d love to make sure I\'m saying your name correctly — is it Pree-ya?',
['Hi — sorry, remind me of your name again?','Hi there! Great to bump into you again.','Hey! So you\'re the analytics person, right?'],
'Using her name immediately and confirming pronunciation shows respect and creates a strong memory hook. People are genuinely touched when someone cares enough to get their name right.',
'Asking her to remind you of her name this soon after meeting signals poor attention. Greeting without her name misses a reinforcement opportunity. Identifying her only by job function feels impersonal.'),
mcq(4,'She confirms the pronunciation and you both head to the project discussion. During the meeting, how do you continue reinforcing her name in your memory?',
'Use her name naturally two or three times when directing questions or comments to her throughout the discussion.',
['Use her name after every single sentence you direct at her.','Write her name on your notepad under the table so you can glance at it whenever needed.','Avoid using her name at all and just make eye contact when you mean to address her.'],
'Using someone\'s name naturally a few times during a conversation embeds it in your memory without feeling forced or overly deliberate.',
'Overusing someone\'s name repeatedly feels unnatural and can come across as insincere. Writing it down can look odd if noticed. Avoiding the name entirely means you lose every reinforcement opportunity.'),
mcq(5,'The meeting wraps up. To lock Priya\'s name in your memory for future reference, what should you do right after she leaves?',
'Jot down "Priya — analytics lead" in your phone notes along with one memorable detail from your conversation as an anchor.',
['Trust that you will remember it since you used her name in the meeting.','Search for her on LinkedIn immediately and send a connection request with no message.','Ask your manager to CC her on emails going forward so her name stays visible.'],
'Writing down a name with a contextual detail immediately after a meeting is one of the most reliable memory techniques and requires only seconds.',
'Relying on memory alone after a busy mixer is risky. A cold LinkedIn request without a note can feel premature. Relying on your manager to manage your name memory is not a sustainable strategy.')],
'response':resp('You navigated forgetting a name gracefully, confirmed pronunciation, used it deliberately, and created a memory anchor. Now type or say aloud the quick note you would save in your phone after the event to remember Priya and the context of your conversation.',
'Impressive! Remembering names is one of the highest-impact social skills you can build. Your system of linking names to details will serve you in every professional and personal setting.')})

levels.append({'id':6,'journey_id':1,'journey_title':'Social Foundations','title':'Reading Body Language',
'scenario':'You are having coffee with a colleague, Martina, to catch up. She said she wanted to talk but since sitting down she has given short answers, crossed her arms, broken eye contact frequently, and kept glancing at her phone. Something seems off, and you need to read the situation and respond appropriately.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'Martina\'s body language suggests something is wrong. What is the best first response?',
'Pause the small talk and gently say, "Hey, you seem a bit distracted — is everything okay? We can reschedule if now isn\'t a great time."',
['Keep talking enthusiastically about your own updates and hope she warms up.','Ask her directly, "Why are you being so cold with me?"','Ignore the signals and carry on as if nothing is different.'],
'Acknowledging the signals you are reading and offering a graceful out shows emotional intelligence and communicates that you are attuned to her — not just the agenda.',
'Powering through your own updates ignores her clear discomfort. Directly labelling her behaviour as "cold" can feel accusatory. Ignoring the signals entirely sends the message that you are not paying attention.'),
mcq(2,'She says she is fine but still looks tense and avoids eye contact. How do you respond?',
'Okay — I\'m here if you want to talk about anything. No pressure at all.',
['You clearly are not fine — I can tell something is wrong.','Fine, then let\'s just get through what we need to cover.','Should I be worried? Did I do something to upset you?'],
'Offering an open, low-pressure invitation without pushing honours her boundary while keeping the door open for her to share when she is ready.',
'Insisting you can tell she is not fine puts her on the defensive. Retreating to a businesslike tone feels cold. Making it about yourself by asking if you did something shifts the focus unhelpfully.'),
mcq(3,'After a short silence, Martina uncrosses her arms and says she had a difficult morning at home. Her posture is starting to open up. How do you respond?',
'I\'m sorry to hear that — home stuff can really colour the whole day. Do you want to talk about it or would you rather keep things light today?',
['Oh thank goodness — I was worried it was something I did.','Home and work really shouldn\'t mix — you need to compartmentalise better.','I completely understand. My mornings are chaos too, let me tell you.'],
'Acknowledging her experience and giving her the choice of how to proceed respects her agency and matches your approach to her actual needs.',
'Expressing relief that it is not about you shifts the focus to your anxiety. Offering unsolicited advice about compartmentalising is preachy. Pivoting to your own story redirects away from her just as she is opening up.'),
mcq(4,'She chooses to share briefly and starts to look more relaxed — making eye contact, leaning slightly in. How do you physically respond?',
'Lean in slightly yourself, maintain comfortable eye contact, and nod gently to show you are fully engaged.',
['Lean back and cross your own arms to give her space.','Pull out your phone to take a note of what she is sharing.','Keep your posture exactly as it was — any change might seem calculated.'],
'Subtly mirroring open, engaged body language helps the other person feel safe and connected. Natural mirroring deepens rapport in good conversations.',
'Leaning back and crossing arms sends a signal of withdrawal right as she is opening up. Taking notes on a personal share feels clinical and invasive. Staying robotically still misses the chance to physically signal your engagement.'),
mcq(5,'The conversation has recovered well and Martina seems much more at ease. How do you wrap up the coffee?',
'I\'m really glad we did this today. Hope the rest of your day gets better — let\'s do this again soon.',
['See, I told you talking about it would help!','Next time, maybe let me know if you\'re having a rough day so we can plan better.','So, back to what I was going to tell you about my week — do you have time?'],
'A warm, affirming close that expresses care for the rest of her day and enthusiasm for reconnecting ends on exactly the right emotional note.',
'Saying "I told you" is self-congratulatory. Suggesting she communicate better next time is mildly critical. Pivoting back to your own agenda after a vulnerable moment feels self-centred.')],
'response':resp('You read Martina\'s closed body language, responded with patience and sensitivity, and watched her gradually open up as you adjusted your approach. Now type or say aloud the text you would send Martina later that afternoon to check in on her.',
'Excellent! Reading body language and adapting your communication in real time is a rare and powerful skill. Your attentiveness today likely meant more to Martina than any words could express.')})

levels.append({'id':7,'journey_id':1,'journey_title':'Social Foundations','title':'Ending Conversations Gracefully',
'scenario':'You are at a neighbour\'s garden party and have been chatting with one of their friends for almost forty minutes. The conversation has been pleasant but has naturally wound down — you are both repeating earlier points and glancing around occasionally. You want to wrap up gracefully without making it feel like a rejection.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'The conversation has clearly run its course but neither of you has made a move to end it. What is the best first signal to give?',
'Let a natural lull happen, then shift your body slightly and say, "It\'s been so great chatting with you."',
['Bluntly announce, "I think we\'ve covered everything, haven\'t we?"','Wait for them to end it — it would be rude to be the one to leave.','Fake a phone buzz and pretend you have an urgent message.'],
'Using a natural pause and a warm affirming phrase is the gentlest and most gracious way to signal the close. It honours the conversation before ending it.',
'Bluntly summarising that you have covered everything sounds like a business wrap-up. Waiting indefinitely makes both people more uncomfortable. Faking a message is dishonest and often obvious.'),
mcq(2,'You have said it has been great chatting. They seem receptive. How do you move toward the actual goodbye?',
'I\'m going to grab another drink and circulate a bit — but I really enjoyed hearing about your travels. Enjoy the party!',
['Okay, I think I should mingle — bye!','I need to find the host — it was nice talking.','This has been fun. Let me give you my number in case you are ever in the area.'],
'Naming a specific natural next action and referencing something they shared — their travels — makes the close feel warm and personal rather than dismissive.',
'"Okay, bye" loses the warmth built over the conversation. Using the host as a transparent excuse feels like a dodge. Offering your number on a first meeting at a party can feel premature.'),
mcq(3,'They add, "Before you go — have you tried the new restaurant on Fifth?" How do you handle this extension without getting pulled back in?',
'I haven\'t yet — is it good? Give me the one-line review and I\'ll add it to my list.',
['Oh, a new restaurant? Tell me everything — I love finding new places.','I\'ll be honest, I\'m not a huge food person, so probably not.','Walking away before they finish speaking.'],
'Acknowledging the topic enthusiastically but capping it at a quick exchange lets them feel heard without reopening the full conversation.',
'Saying "tell me everything" re-anchors the conversation for potentially another twenty minutes. Deflecting with disinterest in food can seem dismissive. Walking away mid-sentence is outright rude.'),
mcq(4,'They give you the quick restaurant review. How do you use this to make the final goodbye feel complete?',
'Perfect — I\'m going to try it. Well, enjoy the rest of the party and it was really lovely meeting you.',
['Thanks! I\'ll Google it later — then awkwardly linger without saying goodbye.','Great, I\'ll tell you what I think next time — are you at these events often?','Thanks — and one more thing before I forget — then start a new topic.'],
'Thanking them for the tip, committing to try the restaurant, and giving a warm farewell wraps everything up cleanly without extending further.',
'Lingering in silence after thanking them creates an awkward limbo. Asking when you will see them again reopens the connection loop. Starting a new topic right before leaving is the opposite of ending gracefully.'),
mcq(5,'As you walk away, they call after you saying it was really great meeting you. How do you respond in the moment?',
'Turn back briefly with a genuine smile and say, "You too — really! Take care!"',
['Wave without turning around and keep walking.','Stop, turn around, and walk back over to continue the conversation.','Give a thumbs up and say "Yeah, sure!" while still moving away.'],
'Turning back, making brief eye contact, smiling, and returning a warm farewell is the perfect final note — present, genuine, and complete.',
'Waving while walking away without turning feels dismissive of their genuine warmth. Walking back over undoes the graceful close you just executed. A thumbs up and a casual "yeah sure" feels flippant.')],
'response':resp('You ended a naturally winding-down conversation with warmth, honesty, and grace — no fake excuses, no awkward lingering. Now type or say aloud the exact words you used to respond when they called after you.',
'Superb! Ending conversations gracefully is an underrated skill. Leaving people with a warm, complete feeling is what makes them remember you fondly and look forward to the next conversation.')})

levels.append({'id':8,'journey_id':1,'journey_title':'Social Foundations','title':'Asking Good Questions',
'scenario':'You are at dinner with four acquaintances — people you know but have not spent much time with individually. The conversation has been pleasant but shallow, skimming topics without going deeper. You want to use the power of thoughtful questions to move the evening from surface-level chat to something genuinely memorable.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'The table is discussing weekend plans in a fairly surface way. How do you introduce a question that moves the conversation deeper?',
'Ask, "I\'m curious — what\'s one thing any of you have been genuinely excited about recently? Could be anything at all."',
['Ask, "So what does everyone do for work?"','Ask, "Did anyone catch the game last weekend?"','Ask, "What are everyone\'s plans for the long weekend?"'],
'An open, personal question that invites enthusiasm and is not tied to any single topic creates space for authentic, unexpected answers that deepen conversation.',
'Asking about work is the default move that rarely produces memorable answers. Sports questions exclude those who are not interested. Asking about weekend plans continues the shallow track the table is already on.'),
mcq(2,'One person, David, says he\'s been obsessed with learning to ferment his own hot sauce. How do you follow up in a way that encourages him to go deeper?',
'That\'s so specific and cool — what made you decide hot sauce, of all things, was the thing you wanted to master?',
['Fermentation is so trendy right now, isn\'t it?','Oh interesting. So is anyone else working on a hobby?','Does it actually taste good? I\'m sceptical about homemade sauces.'],
'Asking about the "why" behind someone\'s passion invites a personal and often surprising story — exactly the kind of depth that makes dinners memorable.',
'Calling it trendy can feel diminishing of his personal effort. Immediately redirecting to the rest of the group cuts off his story. Expressing scepticism about his hobby is discouraging.'),
mcq(3,'David shares an enthusiastic story about how hot sauce-making started from his grandmother\'s kitchen. The rest of the table has leaned in. How do you keep the group energy high?',
'Turn to the group and ask, "Does anyone else have a skill or tradition they picked up from a family member?"',
['Share a long story of your own about a family tradition.','Ask David if he sells the sauce or just makes it for personal use.','Say, "Love that — anyway, who wants dessert?" and signal the waiter.'],
'Linking David\'s answer to a broader question for the table takes his story and uses it as a bridge to bring everyone in, creating a richer shared conversation.',
'Launching into your own long story right after his redirects the spotlight. Asking a logistical follow-up deflates the emotional moment. Jumping to dessert ends the depth just as it was getting interesting.'),
mcq(4,'A quieter guest, Yuna, tentatively starts sharing about a pottery technique she learned from her mother. She seems unsure whether people are interested. How do you draw her out?',
'Lean toward her slightly and say, "Yuna, I\'d love to hear more — what does it actually feel like to make something with your hands like that?"',
['Nod politely and wait for her to finish so you can move to the next topic.','Jump in with, "Oh, I\'ve always wanted to try pottery — it looks so therapeutic."','Ask, "Do you sell your work anywhere?"'],
'A question that targets the sensory and emotional experience — "what does it feel like" — shows deep interest and encourages someone hesitant to share more fully.',
'Politely nodding while waiting to move on communicates indifference. Immediately bringing the focus back to your own pottery interest hijacks her moment. A logistical question about selling shifts away from the experience she is describing.'),
mcq(5,'The dinner is ending and the group agrees it was one of the best evenings they\'ve had together. How do you close the night with a question that leaves people with something to think about?',
'Ask, "Last question — if you could spend one year getting truly masterful at something with no constraints, what would it be?"',
['Ask, "Same time next month?" and start putting on your coat.','Say, "Great night — we should do this more often" and leave it there.','Ask, "So what is everyone up to this week?"'],
'A final thought-provoking question gives the group a beautiful, memorable moment to end on — something they will likely still be thinking about on the drive home.',
'Jumping straight to logistics skips the emotional close of a meaningful evening. "We should do this more often" is a pleasant platitude but leaves no lasting impression. Asking about this week is too mundane to close a profound conversation.')],
'response':resp('You transformed a shallow group dinner into a genuine, memorable evening by asking thoughtful, open, emotionally resonant questions. Now type or say aloud the one question from tonight that you would save to use again at the next dinner party you attend.',
'Brilliant! Great questions are the engine of great conversations. You showed that a single well-placed question can shift an entire room from small talk to something that truly connects people.')})

levels.append({'id':9,'journey_id':1,'journey_title':'Social Foundations','title':'Showing Genuine Interest',
'scenario':'You are at your partner\'s work event, surrounded by their colleagues — people you have never met. You want to make a good impression not by talking about yourself, but by showing genuine curiosity and interest in the people around you. Your goal is to leave having made at least one person feel truly seen and valued.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You approach a colleague of your partner\'s who is standing alone with a drink. How do you open in a way that signals genuine interest?',
'Hi, I\'m [Name] — I don\'t think we\'ve met. How long have you been at the company?',
['Hi, I\'m just trying to make conversation while my partner is busy — mind if I stand here?','Oh hi — do you know where the food table is?','Hi — so, what do you do here?'],
'Introducing yourself warmly and asking an easy personal question about their tenure shows you are there to connect, not just to fill a social gap.',
'Opening by framing yourself as a bored bystander undercuts genuine connection. Using them as a directory for the food table is purely transactional. "What do you do here" as an opener often produces rehearsed, flat answers.'),
mcq(2,'They say they have been with the company for eight years and clearly light up talking about how much has changed. How do you show you are genuinely engaged?',
'Eight years — that\'s incredible. What has been the biggest shift you\'ve seen in that time?',
['Wow, that\'s a long time. You must be pretty senior then.','Interesting! My partner joined about six months ago — have you two worked together much?','Eight years is a long time to stay anywhere these days, isn\'t it?'],
'Reflecting their milestone back with a thoughtful follow-up question about their experience shows you are listening to them as a person — not just gathering facts.',
'Linking it immediately to seniority makes it transactional. Pivoting to ask about your partner makes you seem interested in networking for your partner\'s benefit. Implying eight years is unusually long can feel subtly critical.'),
mcq(3,'They share passionately about how the company\'s culture has evolved. You genuinely find this interesting. How do you express interest without sounding sycophantic?',
'That\'s a really nuanced perspective — having that long-term view must give you a very different lens than someone newer.',
['That\'s so fascinating! You\'re so insightful!','Wow, sounds like quite a journey — anyway, do you know many other people here tonight?','I\'ve read a lot about culture change in organisations — want me to share some thoughts?'],
'Reflecting on the value of their specific vantage point shows genuine appreciation for their insight in a grounded, specific way.',
'Over-the-top praise sounds hollow. Pivoting to networking redirects away from them just as they are engaged. Offering to share your own research shifts the spotlight entirely to you.'),
mcq(4,'They ask what you do for work. You answer briefly. They seem curious and ask a follow-up. How do you answer while keeping the conversation mutually engaging?',
'Answer in two or three sentences and then ask if the work their company does has ever crossed over with anything in your field.',
['Give a full five-minute explanation of your career history, accomplishments, and future plans.','Say, "Oh, it\'s a bit boring — let\'s stay on your story, it\'s much more interesting."','Answer and then go quiet, waiting for them to ask more.'],
'Answering briefly and bridging back with a curious, relevant question keeps the exchange reciprocal and signals that you value conversation over monologue.',
'A long career monologue turns the conversation into a presentation. Deflecting with false modesty can feel performative. Going quiet after answering puts all the social labour on them.'),
mcq(5,'Your partner comes to find you and it is time to move on. This person says warmly that they really enjoyed talking with you. How do you respond in a way that makes the moment genuine?',
'Likewise — honestly, your perspective on the company\'s evolution was one of the highlights of the evening for me. I hope we cross paths again.',
['Ha — you\'re just saying that! But thanks.','Yes, it was fun. Well, my partner is waiting — got to run!','Me too! I\'m great at talking to people — it\'s one of my things.'],
'Receiving their warmth sincerely and echoing something specific they shared shows the interest you expressed was genuine, not performative.',
'Deflecting their compliment rejects their sincerity. Rushing off immediately after they expressed warmth undercuts the closing moment. Crediting your own social skill makes it oddly self-focused.')],
'response':resp('You walked into a room full of strangers and chose to be truly curious about one person — and they felt it. Now type or say aloud the message you would send your partner on the way home describing the most interesting thing you learned from that conversation.',
'Wonderful! Genuine interest is the most powerful social tool you have. People can always tell the difference between someone who is curious and someone who is performing curiosity — and you showed the real thing tonight.')})

levels.append({'id':10,'journey_id':1,'journey_title':'Social Foundations','title':'Building Rapport',
'scenario':'You have just started working with a new team at your company after an internal transfer. Your teammates are warm but clearly close-knit, with years of shared history. You want to build authentic rapport quickly without seeming like you are trying too hard or positioning yourself as a permanent outsider looking in.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'It is your first week on the team. How do you begin building rapport in a way that feels natural rather than forced?',
'Be consistently present — greet people by name each morning, ask how their weekend was, and listen to the answers with real attention.',
['Bring in elaborate homemade treats every day to make yourself memorable.','Ask the team lead for a formal briefing on each team member so you can seem informed.','Mirror the most popular person on the team in everything they do so the group associates you with them.'],
'Consistent, warm, low-key presence is how rapport is actually built — through many small moments of genuine attention, not through grand gestures.',
'Daily treats can feel like you are trying to buy acceptance. Obtaining briefings on people to seem informed is manipulative. Mirroring someone else\'s persona is inauthentic and unsustainable.'),
mcq(2,'A colleague, Tarek, mentions that the team has a long-running tradition of a Friday lunch. He seems slightly hesitant to invite you. How do you respond?',
'Oh I\'d love to join — if you\'ll have me? I don\'t want to crash something that\'s a team tradition without being invited properly.',
['Great — where do you usually go? I\'ll just come along this Friday.','Oh, I don\'t want to intrude — maybe once I\'ve settled in more.','I\'ll let the team lead know I\'m interested in joining so it\'s been flagged officially.'],
'Expressing genuine enthusiasm while checking that you are welcome respects the team\'s existing culture and shows you value belonging through invitation rather than assumption.',
'Assuming you are welcome without confirmation can feel presumptuous. Deferring indefinitely signals you are not interested in the social fabric of the team. Routing through the team lead for a lunch invitation is unnecessarily formal.'),
mcq(3,'At Friday lunch, the team is reminiscing about a past project you were not part of. How do you engage without pretending to share an experience you did not have?',
'Smile, listen genuinely, and when there is a natural pause ask, "What made that project so memorable — what went right or wrong?"',
['Stay quiet the whole time so as not to insert yourself into a shared memory.','Try to relate by saying, "That sounds exactly like a project I was on before — let me tell you about it."','Laugh along at the references even if you do not understand them.'],
'Asking a genuine question about their shared experience shows interest in their history and invites them to share it with you, organically drawing you into their world.',
'Staying completely quiet for a prolonged period signals disengagement. Immediately pivoting to your own parallel story can feel competitive. Laughing at references you do not understand risks seeming fake and is easily noticed.'),
mcq(4,'After a few weeks, a colleague asks your opinion on how the team could improve its weekly meetings. How do you share your perspective as a newcomer without seeming presumptuous?',
'I have a thought — but I\'m aware I\'m still new, so tell me if this misses context. It seems like a clear agenda sent ahead of time could help. Is that something that\'s been tried before?',
['Honestly, the meetings need a complete overhaul — here are seven things I\'d fix.','I\'m still too new to really comment — ask me in six months.','I think the meetings are fine, actually — I don\'t want to rock the boat.'],
'Framing your perspective with humility, offering a single specific suggestion, and checking if it has been tried shows you are thoughtful and collaborative rather than arrogant.',
'A list of seven fixes from a new team member can feel tone-deaf. Refusing to contribute any view misses a genuine invitation to engage. Pretending you have no opinion signals a lack of initiative.'),
mcq(5,'After a month, a teammate tells you that the team really likes having you. How do you respond in a way that deepens the rapport?',
'That genuinely means a lot to me — I\'ve really enjoyed getting to know everyone. This team has a great energy and I feel lucky to be part of it.',
['Ha, well I do tend to get on with most people pretty quickly.','Thanks! I\'ve been working really hard to fit in, so that\'s a relief.','Oh, stop it — you\'re just being nice.'],
'Receiving the warmth sincerely, expressing genuine positive feelings about the team, and acknowledging their culture deepens the bond and shows that connection has been mutual.',
'Attributing it to your general social ability feels boastful and deflects the specific warmth they are expressing. "That\'s a relief" makes the interaction sound anxiety-driven. Dismissing it as politeness rejects their genuine sentiment.')],
'response':resp('You built real rapport with a new team through consistency, curiosity, humility, and genuine warmth — week by week, moment by moment. Now type or say aloud the message you would send the teammate who told you the team really likes having you.',
'Outstanding! Rapport is not built in a day — it grows through dozens of small, genuine moments. You approached your new team with authenticity and patience, and it paid off. This is the foundation of every strong professional relationship.')})
